WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.400
Stealing a colleague’s birth control should&nbsp;
get you fired from any normal workplace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.308
but it won’t at the "NCIS" office… and DiNozzo&nbsp;
is just the start of the show’s problems.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.180
Procedural crime dramas have a way of manipulating&nbsp;
the reality-based aspects of their characters'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.340
jobs. In order to fast track some of the scenes&nbsp;
that would normally involve a lab filled with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.560
team of scientists, it sort of makes sense for&nbsp;
a TV show to speed up the forensic process.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.301
In the case of "NCIS," Abby and Ducky's&nbsp;
jobs wouldn't exist in real life.

00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:35.106
"Alright."

00:00:35.106 --> 00:00:40.500
We don't mean that forensic analysts and medical&nbsp;
examiners aren't actual jobs — obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.680
they are. However, a forensic analyst and medical&nbsp;
examiner wouldn't work within an NCIS building;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.680
they wouldn't have a direct line of communication&nbsp;
to an NCIS agent, nor would there only be one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.740
each. A real-life NCIS field office would work&nbsp;
with local state or county medical examiners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.080
and labs for forensic evidence and each one would&nbsp;
certainly have more than one person working there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.660
Gibbs' team also isn't the only group of field&nbsp;
agents that operates in that branch of NCIS.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:15.840
In that case, Abby and Ducky would have to keep up&nbsp;
with Gibbs' cases and every other agent's cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.600
If that were true for real-world counterparts,&nbsp;
the overtime costs alone would be astronomical.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.060
For the sake of television, it's pretty much&nbsp;
a necessity to keep the show's pacing punchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.980
and exciting. Otherwise, each episode&nbsp;
would be a victim of the red-taped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.593
glacial pace of real-time forensic sciences.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.980
In the very first episode of "NCIS,"&nbsp;
titled "Yankee White," the death of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.360
a Marine on Air Force One triggers a&nbsp;
mess of jurisdictional complications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.520
The ultimate question of this episode is&nbsp;
who claims jurisdiction over the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.080
of a Naval commander who dies on Air&nbsp;
Force One? Do Gibbs and his team have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.280
any right to fly away with the president's&nbsp;
plane in order to hold such jurisdiction?

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.840
Ok — let's break this one down. The&nbsp;
second the president walks off the plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.100
the Secret Service loses jurisdiction,&nbsp;
and it gets handed off to the FBI.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.500
But in accordance with SECNAV Instruction,&nbsp;
since NCIS has a horse in this race in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.760
form of a dead officer, "joint criminal&nbsp;
investigations [...] conducted with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.280
FBI [...] designate NCIS as having exclusive&nbsp;
responsibility." So, in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.860
NCIS actually would have jurisdiction&nbsp;
to take custody of the body. But does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.940
that really give them the right to snatch the&nbsp;
president's plane to ensure they keep custody?

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.680
And if they can't take the plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.000
how is it that we never hear about anyone&nbsp;
getting into trouble for that stunt?

00:02:39.594 --> 00:02:44.400
When it comes to forensics, contaminations&nbsp;
are one of the worst mess-ups that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.480
happen to an investigation. It's also&nbsp;
the case that biohazardous materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.620
which can possibly cause illness and&nbsp;
contamination, are frequent fliers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.247
a medical examiner's field. Ducky, who is&nbsp;
supposed to be the best at what he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.324 --> 00:03:02.555
ought to know how not to contaminate a&nbsp;
forensic lab or spread unsanitary materials.

00:03:02.555 --> 00:03:08.628
"Excuse me. Show a little respect,&nbsp;
this is a place of peace and dignity."

00:03:08.628 --> 00:03:14.880
In "Sub Rosa," Ducky potentially contaminates&nbsp;
several areas with biohazardous materials. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.020
meets Abby in her lab after performing&nbsp;
an autopsy. As he enters Abby's area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.580
we can see he is still in his surgical&nbsp;
gown and there's blood smeared all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.500
the front. Blood is considered a biohazard&nbsp;
material, and not only does bringing it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.080
of his lab and into Abby's increase the chances&nbsp;
for an infection to break loose, but it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.060
potentially contaminate any other evidence&nbsp;
that is present in Abby's workspace. Gross.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.100
In other episodes, Ducky, among other&nbsp;
investigators, commits potential crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.420
scene contamination through lack of proper&nbsp;
personal protection equipment. The National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.940
Institute of Justice explains that crime scene&nbsp;
investigators must have items such as "gloves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:59.400
booties, hair covering, overalls, and mask,"&nbsp;
readily available for a crime scene sweep.

00:04:00.932 --> 00:04:05.520
The team encounters their very first&nbsp;
multi-episode "NCIS" villain near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.020
the end of Season 1. By the "NCIS"&nbsp;
Season 2 finale, he's still at large.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:15.360
But we're more curious about why a standard&nbsp;
criminal investigative team would seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.060
be the sole faction charged with taking down&nbsp;
terrorist Ari Haswari. Gibbs and his agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.280
typically take on cases that involve far more&nbsp;
standard crimes than counterterrorism. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:32.100
real-life NCIS does include counterterrorism&nbsp;
efforts, but that wouldn't include a team like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.860
Gibbs'. The "NCIS" group would probably have&nbsp;
to undergo a lot of extra training dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.780
to counterterrorism that would hardly ever come&nbsp;
up in their day-to-day work. Even after all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:48.240
where exactly is the time to get all their other&nbsp;
case work done if they're going after terrorists?

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:54.240
The real-life counterterrorism portions of NCIS&nbsp;
would take on a case like Ari's, and it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.220
probably take years of dedicated work and a lot of&nbsp;
information sharing with other federal branches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.800
It's a safe story arc, especially for the era&nbsp;
in which it was originally aired. In the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.140
following 9/11, counterterrorism and heavily&nbsp;
inflated patriotism flooded the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.815
making this a TV plotline that went&nbsp;
over well with American audiences.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.040
"NCIS" spent some time in hot water&nbsp;
when fans and critics started asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.600
"Is NCIS sexist?" A lot of concerns&nbsp;
over the show's depiction of women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.500
came about because of Michael&nbsp;
Weatherly's character, DiNozzo.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.660
During the first few seasons, DiNozzo and Kate are&nbsp;
constantly bickering about the differences between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:39.367
men and women all while plopping women into&nbsp;
two-dimensional stereotypical roles and behaviors.

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:41.134
"Tony, how long have you been in here?"

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.650
"Long enough to know you can't sing. And&nbsp;
you haven't shaved your legs in a week."

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:50.340
In later seasons, there's a pretty big shift&nbsp;
in DiNozzo and the overall tone of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.120
as audiences grew tired of watching&nbsp;
the same prejudiced banter.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:59.820
Fans noticed a shift in "NCIS" personalities&nbsp;
around Season 9. But instead of it being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.080
a step away from their initial portrayal&nbsp;
in earlier seasons – which would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.400
mean multiple personality rewrites —&nbsp;
the shift was designed to allow the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.388
show's women more depth, as opposed to&nbsp;
the shallowness we'd previously seen.

00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:18.900
Apparently, rules are loose guidelines&nbsp;
when it comes to the "NCIS" workplace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.840
Even someone like DiNozzo should know when to pump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.280
the breaks and start thinking with his&nbsp;
head instead of… his other appendages.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.920
DiNozzo has nearly no filter when it comes to&nbsp;
talking to women, whether it be at work or when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.080
he's off the clock. But since what he does in&nbsp;
his own time is up to him, we're more concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.480
when he's trying to pick up women while working&nbsp;
cases. In "Forced Entry," he and Kate are sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.280
to question a suspect at a base hospital. When&nbsp;
he approaches the desk to ask the Lieutenant on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.154
duty about the suspect, he asks her out to a lunch&nbsp;
date so he can go through some routine questions.

00:06:53.154 --> 00:06:57.134
"This is my engagement ring, Agent&nbsp;
DiNozzo. I brought my lunch."

00:06:57.218 --> 00:07:02.820
Even after the rejection, DiNozzo continues&nbsp;
to push by saying that engaged is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:07.440
technically married and implies she's on&nbsp;
the fence about her wedding. We cringe.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.540
Later, in "Mind Games," he takes a female&nbsp;
colleague's birth control pills and holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.980
them in the air for her to retrieve like she's&nbsp;
a child, all while saying that she couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:23.160
possibly need them unless she's seeing someone.&nbsp;
Not a great look, DiNozzo. Not great at all.

